  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThe upper level pattern still shows a ridge trough type pattern\nwith the trough being over the east and continuing through the\nperiod. At the surface, a stationary boundary remains along\nsouthern Ohio and southeast into WV. This will keep the MCS\ntrack in a northwest to southeast track. The NBM gives a 40%\nchance of rainfall over 1 inch southwest of Pittsburgh through\nthe area. The other concern is that the region in the corridor\nfor continued rainfall is under a region of 1.8 to 2.0 PWAT\nvalues according to the HREF. Not to mention that this is the\nmean result. CAMS suggest that the timing of this rainfall is\nmore in the 14Z to 20Z timeframe. The forecast area remains out\nof the marginal severe risk with the exception of south Tucker\nCounty being clipped for a wind threat. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nGuidance suggests a bit of a break in weather threats as a\nsurface high pressure attempts to push south from Ontario, but\nthis break will be brief before more severe/flood threats \narrive Sunday as the high pushes east. Ensembles generally agree\nin an west-east sliding low. This area may see a slow moving \nwarm front ahead of the system and a slow dragging cold front \nbehind. \n\nThe flatter the flow, the less of a chance for ascent and \nminimization of any threats. But should the upper shortwave \ntrend more amplified, there may be more ascent near the surface \nlow. This would increase excessive rainfall threats along the \nrelatively slow passing fronts and raise convective threats in \nthe warm sector. The conditional flood risk favors the whole \narea with a reasonable worst case scenario (10% chance) of a \nlocalized amount somewhere of 3 to 5 inches in a 48hr period. \nThe conditional severe threat increases from north to south, as \ndoes the distance into the warm sector. The primary threat would\nappear to be damaging wind at this time. The only variable here\nis the timing of the arrival as there is increased support that\nthe MCS will move through late Saturday night.",
